the TV series based on the works of  Stephen King are a mixed bag some are  incredible others not so much so which  ones are worth watching we're leaving  out some big ones here counting only the  TV series or miniseries with three or  more installments with that in mind  here's every Stephen King TV series  ranked from worst to best Stephen King  has long been outspoken of his contempt  for Stanley Kubrick's 1980 adaptation of  The Shining in 2014 he told Rolling  Stone that he considered it not only a  terrible adaptation but a bad movie in  general he explained obviously people  love it and they don't understand why I  don't the book is hot and the movie is  cold the book ends in fire and the movie  in Ice King took particular aim at how  the movie adapted the Wendy character  played with desperate intensity by  Shelley Duvall the writer said of her  portrayal it's so misogynistic  I mean Wendy Torrance is just presented  as the sort of screaming dishrag  Kubrick's adaptation of The Shining  wouldn't be the last 17 years later ABC  released a three Episode miniseries  version of the story starring Stephen  Webber and Rebecca De Mornay over four  and a half hours King was finally able  to give audiences the story he always  wanted to see on screen while it was  reviewed well at the time the years  haven't been very kind to it  [Music]  turns out four and a half hours is far  too much for this particular King story  the series drags feeling bloated to fill  time and Webber place crazy in a way  that's more goofy than frightening the  whole thing plays out like an  overcorrection and it winds up being far  worse than anything Kubrick could have  ever produced the 2002 series rose-red  revolves around a dormant haunted  Seattle mansion psychology professor  Joyce Reardon brings in an unsuspecting  group of would-be psychics to serve as  bait for the house so she can somehow  get back at her department chair for  taking her tenure aside from the house  itself which makes for a decent horror  backdrop everything else about rose-red  Falls totally flat too much time is  wasted on wandering about aimlessly  calling out to each other and well  wandering some more the characters are  all awful stereotypes and Nancy Travis  is horribly miscast as Joyce on all this  miniseries feels more like a wilted rose  than a red one Stephen King's golden  years was actually the first original  piece of work the author created for TV  airing on CBS in 1991 the series  centered around Harlan Williams a  janitor in his 70s who has given strange  powers after a lab experiment goes awry  Harlan is doused in chemicals that not  only reverses aging but in later  episodes given powers that affect the  world around him in spite of it's  supernatural overtones golden years  winds up playing out a lot like a  typical political thriller the onset of  Harlan's powers means that he has to  keep hidden from government agents who  worry their top-secret experiments will  somehow be exposed Harlan is a threat so  he has to figure out not only how to  keep the agency bad guys off his tail  but also how to reverse the effects of  his chemical accident while it may not  be the best thing King has ever made  it's at least entertaining for the most  part luckily the show is also supported  by one of the better casts to have  appeared in a Stephen King series to  Haven ran on Sci Fi for five seasons  between 2010 and 2015 which actually  makes it one of Stephen King's most  commercially successful TV series based  on the author's novel the Colorado kid  Haven follows FBI agent Audrey Parker as  she in  investigate supernatural occurrences  called the troubles in Haven Maine as  time goes on the show delves deeper into  Audrey's mysterious backstory as well as  the source of the troubles Haven took  some time to find its footing and its  first season was the lowest rated of its  five on Rotten Tomatoes  with the Critics consensus being that  the show was a derivative supernatural  crime procedural as time went on however  Haven improved shifting focus on to its  supernatural elements and character  developments rather than following the  standard police procedural formula the  show can go a little off the rails at  times but as a whole it works pretty  well  nightmares &amp; dreamscapes from the  stories of Stephen King is an eight part  anthology series that ran on TNT in 2006  it's based mostly off Kings short story  collection of the same name and each  episode tells a completely different  tale with a completely different cast of  characters in true king fashion  nightmares &amp; dreamscapes acts as a near  perfect representation of the author's  track record with small screen  adaptations some of the episodes are  good others are not the biggest problem  is that nightmares &amp; dreamscapes tries  to do a little bit of everything which  perhaps explains why twines up finding  success only about half the time what it  does manage to do well however is bring  together a solid cast for each episode  and the presence of those cast tends to  make up for some of the show's weaker  storylines overall however nightmares &amp;  dreamscapes only serves as a sad  reminder that you could be spending your  time watching a much better anthology  series in a perfect world Kingdom  hospital would have been a miniseries  instead it was stretched into a regular  TV show that still wound up lasting only  15 episodes it tells the story of a  bizarre staff and patients at Kingdom  Hospital in Lewiston Maine a place that  sits on the ruins of another recently  burned down Hospital the show gets  pretty crazy at times but it's also  genuinely creepy too by far the most  interesting thing about Kingdom Hospital  however is the fact that it's the first  adaptation Stephen King took on that  wasn't drawn from his own material in  fact the show was originally based on a  Danish TV movie by lars von trier  still King told the New York Times that  the show was the thing I like best out  of all the things I've done the mist  could have been a great show about human  nature and the horror that comes with  facing your deepest secrets instead it  goes way too far with its visual effects  and totally forgets that in order to  make an audience care about a character  that character has to be likeable I  think we should think less and pray more  in the case of the mist which only made  it to ten episodes before being  cancelled the characters are generally  pretty terrible people and they really  don't make it easy to root for them add  to that across-the-board mediocrity on  the part of the show's actors and the  result is a series that fails to live up  to the big-screen version that came a  few years before on the plus side the  mist does have one of the creepiest  settings of any Stephen King adaptation  the fictional Bridgeville main is a  beautiful and picturesque location that  morphs into a living nightmare for its  residents apart from that not much to  see here in 1999 ABC aired storm of the  century a three-part miniseries about  the residents of little tall island set  off the coast of Maine when a storm  rolls in that brings the town to a  complete standstill a mysterious and  deadly man named Andre Leno's approaches  the townspeople and promises not to harm  them for a price in a sinister twist  that price turns out to be one of the  families children storm of the century  is another of Stephen King's original  screenplays as opposed to an adapted  work and while it's a far more  successful attempt at the danger in the  weather theme than that portrayed by the  mist its failure lies and king's  inability to self edit there's really no  reason this particular story should have  been a three-part series if anything it  would have made a great feature film but  there's only so far you can go with the  story of a demonic man in search of a  protege especially when the bulk of  people involved in the story are pretty  one-dimensional background characters  under the dome has a fascinating premise  an entire town is suddenly blocked off  from the rest of the world by way of a  huge indestructible dome no one knows  where it came from  the gun  and can't get through it and resources  quickly start to dwindle  while there are sci-fi elements  obviously in place here under the dome  finds its most success on account of  Stephen King's ability to find monsters  in everyday people its first season was  certified fresh' on Rotten Tomatoes with  the Critics consensus being that the  show was an effective and engrossing  horror mystery with airtight plotting  and great special effects but unlike  other kings series under the dome lost  steam going into its second and third  seasons despite a number of twists and  turns in the story it just wasn't enough  story there to warrant the number of  years it stayed on the air like many of  Stephen King's stories the deadzone  begins with a car crash after waking up  from a six-year coma Jonny Smith  suddenly becomes able to see the past  and future of those he comes into  contact with as the deadzone progresses  Jonny becomes more involved with law  enforcement taking on an advisory role  of sorts to help solve crimes this was  in 2002 prior to Haven and so many other  supernatural crime dramas we see on TV  now so at the time the deadzone was a  fairly original concept add that to a  solid cast and the show winds up being a  pretty well-rounded endeavor for its  sixth season run if you were to flip on  the stand now there's a good chance you  wouldn't be able to make it through the  first episode the special effects in  this six hour miniseries are very very  bad firmly cementing it in time as a  product of early 90s television if you  can look past its dodgy effects however  the stand is a series that won't  disappoint the stand follows a journey  of a group of survivors in  post-apocalyptic America this series is  a long haul - it's a four-part  miniseries that completely lays out and  then resolves the battle for Humanity  but so is the Stephen King novel it's  based upon at over 1,100 pages the stand  is one of the author's longest books  ultimately the miniseries does the work  justice but it'll take a little  sacrifice on the viewers part to get  there in 2016  Stephen King teamed up with JJ Abrams to  bring his novel 11/22/63 to life the  resulting series which ran for 8  episodes on hulu tells the story of what  would  happen if someone prevented JFK's  assassination James Franco plays Jake  Epping a teacher who discovers a time  portal in the back room of his friend's  cafe I'm telling you you shouldn't be  here  well  I'm here as Jake attempts to change the  past the past pushes back against his  efforts and things get complicated when  he meets and falls for a woman named  Sadie not only is this a Stephen King  series that doesn't look like a cheap  made-for-tv movie but 11/22/63 is also  well acted and well-written  and it's eight episode length is exactly  the right amount of time to tell this  story without feeling like it's dragging  audiences into the abyss  everything that Stephen King does best  all comes together beautifully in the  little Maine town of Castle Rock in the  creepy Hulu show of the same name  Andre Holland plays Henry Matthew Deaver  a man who returns to his hometown two  decades after leaving having once been  suspected of killing his adopted father  during his stay he uncovers some  disturbing secrets buried by the town's  officials as well as the supernatural  forces behind them Castle Rock has ties  to the wider world of Kings written work  so fans will continually come across  hidden easter eggs and in Universe nods  but more importantly the series is just  really well done the cast is phenomenal  the production value is high and the  result is a genuinely frightening show  that has introduced new generations to  the twisty terrifying world of America's  greatest horror author mr. Mercedes  premiered on the audience network in  2017 the series tells the story of bill  Hodges a retired detective determined to  solve the case of mr. Mercedes a killer  who took the lives of 16 people at a  local job fair when mr. Mercedes sets  his sights on Hodges a dark and twisted  game of cat-and-mouse ensues the show  walks a fine line between police drama  mystery and horror and the further along  it gets into its narrative the more  sinister the plot becomes its premiere  season is certified fresh' on Rotten  Tomatoes with the Critics consensus  being that it propels it's tense creepy  narrative with quick-witted dialogue  strong characters and terrifying  surprises check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite shows are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  you  [Music]

